#!/usr/bin/env python3
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# veltrix-prayer-lock — the prayer-time "Go pray" overlay + 60-second lock.
#
#   veltrix-prayer-lock --prayer Fajr [--duration 60] [--ends-at <epoch>]
#                      [--headless] [--state-dir DIR]
#
# SAFETY — READ THIS BEFORE CHANGING ANYTHING. A forced lock that can strand
# the user is unacceptable, so the unlock is guaranteed BY CONSTRUCTION, in
# three independent layers, and NONE of them is "the shell will call unlock":
#
#   1. THIS PROCESS owns the timer. It is spawned by the shell but immediately
#      calls os.setsid() (own session, own process group), so killing the
#      shell — or the shell crashing — cannot signal or reap it. The shell
#      has no handle on the lock's lifetime at all: it fires and forgets.
#      The lock ends when the deadline passes, full stop. The deadline is
#      tracked TWO ways and the lock ends at whichever comes FIRST: the
#      wall-clock instant (--ends-at epoch, or now + --duration) AND a
#      monotonic deadline (time.monotonic() + the same span). A backward
#      clock jump therefore cannot stretch the lock — the monotonic leg
#      ends it on time (fix pass #1, M1). A machine that SLEEPS through the
#      deadline wakes to a lock that is already past its end: the process
#      exits at once, no overlay. (Monotonic advances across suspend on
#      Linux, so the sleep-through behaviour is preserved.)
#
#   2. A WATCHDOG child (fork()ed at start) watches the parent every 0.5 s
#      and enforces the same two deadlines: at the earlier one it SIGTERMs
#      this process, waits 2 s (monotonic), and SIGKILLs it if it is still
#      alive. If the main loop ever hangs (a stuck GTK frame, a deadlocked
#      timeout), the watchdog still ends the lock on time; if the parent dies
#      first (clean unlock, SIGKILL → fail-open, crash), the watchdog removes
#      any stale state file and exits. The watchdog verifies /proc/<pid>/
#      cmdline still names this program AND /proc/<pid>/stat start time
#      still matches the recorded one before it signals anything, so a
#      reused PID — or an `exec -a` argv spoof — can never get a wrong
#      process killed (fix pass #1, A7).
#
#   3. The deadline is an INSTANT re-checked every 250 ms — never a counted
#      number of ticks, so a stalled clock or a suspended machine cannot
#      stretch the lock.
#
# LOCK LENGTH IS CAPPED (fix pass #1, M2): --duration > 300 s, or --ends-at
# more than 300 s in the future, is refused (exit 2). The "60 s bound" is
# true of THIS program, not just of its caller: the shell passes 60, but a
# bad or malicious caller cannot lock the screen for an hour.
#
# EMERGENCY OVERRIDE (fix pass #1, halal + security ruling — the opt-out
# question from the v1 report §4 is CLOSED by this): holding Escape for 3
# continuous seconds exits the lock early. A single press does nothing; a
# 1-second hold does nothing; the hold must be deliberate and is hard to hit
# by accident. The hint is printed ON the overlay so the escape is
# discoverable in the moment it matters ("Hold Esc for 3 seconds to exit in
# an emergency"). The no-skip default is unchanged — Adnan asked for a lock,
# not a reminder — the override is recourse for a wrong-time or emergency
# lock only, and every early exit is logged to stderr. There is still no
# button, no click handler, no D-Bus method, no IPC. The ONLY other exits
# are the deadline, the watchdog, SIGTERM/SIGINT (fail-open: a kill always
# frees the desktop), or SIGKILL.
#
# STUCK-OVERLAY RECOVERY (for the user, in plain words — also in
# docs/prayer-lock-fix-report-1.md): if the overlay is somehow stuck, press
# Ctrl+Alt+F2 to switch to a text console, log in, and run
# `killall veltrix-prayer-lock` — the desktop returns at once (the lock
# fail-opens on SIGTERM). Then Ctrl+Alt+F1 (or F7) returns to the desktop.
# If even that is unavailable, the lock still ends by itself: the monotonic
# deadline cannot be stretched by a clock change, and the watchdog kills
# the process at the deadline.
#
# Input blocking, best-effort ladder (Wayland cannot be grabbed from a plain
# client the way X11 could; the compositor decides). Tried in order:
#   a. ext-session-lock-v1 via Gtk4SessionLock — the compositor enforces the
#      lock: no keybind, no Alt-Tab, no other surface can take input. This
#      is the intended path on the Dell (veltrix-lock already uses it).
#      DELL-GATED: the typelib is not present in this dev VM, so this path
#      has never run here — verify on the Dell before calling it done.
#   b. gtk4-layer-shell OVERLAY layer + exclusive keyboard mode.
#   c. A plain fullscreen window (keep-above + focus). Still shows the
#      overlay and still exits on time, but a determined keybind could
#      switch away — the timer still ends the lock, nothing is stranded.
#   d. Headless (no display at all, or --headless): no window, the timer +
#      watchdog + state file still run. Used by the test harness and as a
#      last resort; it does not block input, and says so on stderr.
# Only ONE rung is ever active: _build_session_lock's on_failed destroys
# its windows before falling back to fullscreen, and the builders refuse to
# run while another rung's windows exist (fix pass #1, code #12).
#
# One lock at a time: the state file $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/veltrix-prayer-lock.json
# (or --state-dir) records {prayer, ends_at, pid, pid_start_time}. A second
# invocation while a live lock's deadline is still in the future exits 2
# without touching anything — a lock can never be extended by re-spawning.
# A stale file is removed, not fatal: a corrupt/unparseable file, a dead
# pid, or a pid that is alive but is NOT a lock process (cmdline AND start
# time checked) all count as stale (fix pass #1, A6). The claim is taken
# atomically (O_CREAT|O_EXCL), so two simultaneous invocations cannot both
# become live locks.
#
# Visuals: full-screen, the REAL wallpaper dimmed and blurred behind the
# text (following ui/lockscreen/widget/Lock.ts — resolve the wallpaper,
# paint it, scrim it for legibility), cyan-only accent (#00E5FF), large
# prayer name leading in the locale's own language (Arabic leads in the ar
# locale, Latin leads in en/es) plus the other language beneath it, a large
# 0:59-style countdown numeral with the sentence under it, and the note
# "The screen unlocks automatically." No blue/purple, no music-player
# styling, no imagery of people (halal rubric §1). Arabic in Noto Naskh
# Arabic; Latin in Inter (Space Grotesk for the hero name). The cursor is
# hidden over the overlay. Strings are GENERATED from the shell locale keys
# `prayerlock.*` by scripts/dev/gen-prayer-lock-strings.py — do not
# hand-edit the tables below; the harness (T7) fails on drift.
#
# Prayer times come from bin/veltrix-prayer-times (ISNA default, mosque
# calibration Dhuhr −1 / Asr +2 — see that file's header for the diagnosis).
# The shell passes --duration 60 only (NOT --ends-at): the lock runs
# [spawn, spawn+60], up to ~6 min after the prayer time if the poll lands
# at the tail of the 5-minute grace window.
#
# Exit codes: 0 = locked for the full duration and unlocked on time (or the
# deadline was already past, so nothing was shown); 1 = could not start
# (bad state dir, unparseable args handled by argparse as 2); 2 = a live lock
# is already running / bad --prayer / bad --duration / lock too long.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

import argparse
import json
import os
import signal
import sys
import time

DEFAULT_DURATION = 60
MAX_LOCK_SECONDS = 300  # absolute cap: no lock may run longer than this (M2)
ESC_HOLD_SECONDS = 3.0  # hold Escape this long to exit early (deliberate)
STATE_FILENAME = "veltrix-prayer-lock.json"

